  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "What does property of a material mean?",
      "a": "A property is what a material is like: hard or soft, bendy or stiff, see-through or not, waterproof or not. We choose materials for their properties.",
      "asks": [
        "What are the properties of materials?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why are windows made of glass?",
      "a": "Glass is transparent, so light comes in and we can see out. It is also hard, waterproof and keeps out wind and rain.",
      "asks": [
        "Why not make windows from wood?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why is an umbrella waterproof?",
      "a": "An umbrella is made of a waterproof material such as nylon or polyester with a coating, so rain runs off instead of soaking through, and a metal frame keeps it stiff.",
      "asks": [
        "What is an umbrella made of?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why are hammers made of metal?",
      "a": "The head of a hammer must be hard, strong and heavy to hit nails without breaking or bending, so steel is perfect. The handle is often wood or rubber for grip.",
      "asks": [
        "What makes a good hammer?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Can one object use several materials?",
      "a": "Yes. A pencil has wood, graphite, metal and a rubber eraser. A bike uses metal for the frame, rubber for the tyres and plastic for the seat.",
      "asks": [
        "Why do things use more than one material?"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "facts": [
    "Spider silk is, weight for weight, stronger than steel.",
    "Glass is transparent because light passes through it without being scattered much."
  ],
